# Hindsight: project rules

Source of truth for scope and design: docs/HINDSIGHT_BUILD_SPEC.md. Read the relevant section before coding. Do not add features outside the spec without asking.

## Stack
Java 21, Spring Boot 4.1.x, Gradle 9.x Kotlin DSL multi-module, Kafka, Postgres (Flyway), Redis, CEL (cel-java), Testcontainers, JUnit 5, Mockito, jqwik.

## Hard rules
- The decision evaluation function is PURE and deterministic. No clocks, randomness, or I/O inside evaluation. Time arrives via the input snapshot.
- Approved policy versions are immutable. Identity is the SHA-256 of canonical JSON.
- Every Kafka consumer is idempotent. Producers use the transactional outbox.
- Audit log is append-only and hash-chained. App DB role has INSERT and SELECT only on audit tables.
- Never use hashCode() for anything persisted or routed. Use a stable SHA-256 based function.
- No secrets in code, config, Helm values, or Terraform. Use env vars and K8s Secrets.
- Containers run non-root, read-only root filesystem, arbitrary UID safe.
- All data is synthetic. Never claim regulatory compliance in code or docs.

## Workflow
- Work on ONE phase or task at a time. Plan first, list files you will touch, then implement.
- Every change includes tests in the same change. Money-of-the-system logic (evaluation, hashing, bucketing, state machine) also gets jqwik property tests.
- Run the tests after changes and fix failures before moving on. Do not disable or weaken tests to make them pass.
- Do NOT run git commit or git push yourself. The developer commits manually from the terminal. Never add Co-authored-by or any attribution trailers to commit messages.
- Small, focused changes. Suggest a conventional commit message (feat:, fix:, test:, refactor:, docs:, chore:) at the end of each task so the developer can use it.
- When you hit a real bug, add an entry to docs/bugs.md (symptom, root cause, fix).
- Prefer simple, readable code over clever code. Comments explain WHY, not WHAT.
- If something in the spec is ambiguous or seems wrong, stop and ask instead of guessing.
